1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,042
Welcome.

2
00:00:04,042 --> 00:00:11,294
In this course we'll take the OpenBCI Cyton board, and its add-on, the Daisy module, apart, piece by piece.

3
00:00:11,294 --> 00:00:17,528
Together they turn the tiny electrical activity of your brain into numbers on your computer:

4
00:00:17,528 --> 00:00:20,985
8 channels with the Cyton alone, 16 with the Daisy.

5
00:00:20,985 --> 00:00:23,831
You don't need any electronics background.

6
00:00:23,831 --> 00:00:33,590
We'll use a 3D model built from OpenBCI's own design files, plus the real schematics, and follow the electricity as it flows through the boards.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,397
Here's the big picture.

8
00:00:36,397 --> 00:00:46,186
Electrodes on your scalp pick up voltages of just 10 to 100 microvolts, tens of thousands of times smaller than the voltage of an AA battery.

9
00:00:46,186 --> 00:00:56,669
The Cyton protects those signals, amplifies them, turns them into numbers 250 times a second, and sends them by radio to a USB dongle on your computer.

10
00:00:56,669 --> 00:01:03,611
We'll follow five flows: power, the brain signal, the bias loop, the digital control, and the radio.

11
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:08,610
Then we'll add the Daisy, and see how two boards stay perfectly in sync.

12
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:18,159
The Cyton is an octagon about 6 centimetres across, with parts on both sides.

13
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:19,457
Let's start on top.

14
00:01:19,457 --> 00:01:27,107
Along the top edge is the electrode header, a double row of right-angle pins where your electrode wires plug in.

15
00:01:27,107 --> 00:01:33,050
Just below it are small black resistor arrays, part of the protection on every channel.

16
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:39,634
In the middle sits the most important chip: the ADS1299 from Texas Instruments.

17
00:01:39,634 --> 00:01:45,817
It's an 8-channel amplifier and analog-to-digital converter, designed specifically for EEG.

18
00:01:45,817 --> 00:01:52,747
Around it are lots of small capacitors that keep its power supplies and its internal reference steady.

19
00:01:53,933 --> 00:02:01,206
Below it is the PIC32 microcontroller, the board's brain, with its 8 megahertz crystal next to it.

20
00:02:01,206 --> 00:02:09,073
Two small buttons, RST and PROG, let you restart it or load new firmware, and a blue LED shows its status.

21
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:12,804
Around the edges are black sockets.

22
00:02:12,804 --> 00:02:15,341
J1, on the left, is the Daisy port.

23
00:02:15,341 --> 00:02:19,256
J3 and J4 bring out spare pins of the microcontroller.

24
00:02:19,256 --> 00:02:29,767
Near the bottom is a tiny accelerometer that measures head movement, and next to it an even tinier logic chip, an OR gate, that we'll meet later.

25
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:32,529
Now flip the board over.

26
00:02:32,529 --> 00:02:39,711
On the underside are the battery plug, the power supply chips, the radio module, and the micro SD card slot.

27
00:02:39,711 --> 00:02:44,365
Right under the electrode pins you'll also find more protection parts:

28
00:02:44,365 --> 00:02:46,493
TVS diodes and capacitor arrays.

29
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:51,792
On one edge is the slide switch, with three positions:

30
00:02:51,792 --> 00:02:53,014
PC, OFF and BLE.

31
00:02:53,014 --> 00:03:09,055
The schematic, the circuit diagram OpenBCI published, groups all these parts into the same blocks: power supply, input protection, the ADS1299, the microcontroller, the radio, the SD card and the accelerometer.

32
00:03:09,055 --> 00:03:12,187
We'll go through them one flow at a time.

33
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,097
A quick word on how to read a schematic.

34
00:03:16,097 --> 00:03:20,615
Each chip is drawn as a box with its pins numbered around the edge.

35
00:03:20,615 --> 00:03:21,694
Lines are wires.

36
00:03:21,694 --> 00:03:26,482
A dot where lines cross means they're connected; no dot, no connection.

37
00:03:26,482 --> 00:03:38,283
Instead of drawing every long wire, the schematic often uses names, like DVDD or MISO: every point with the same name is connected, even if it's on the other side of the page.

38
00:03:38,283 --> 00:03:45,161
Parts have reference names, like U6 or R5, and the same name is printed next to the part on the board.

39
00:03:45,161 --> 00:03:48,600
That's how you match the drawing to the real thing.

40
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:57,710
Underneath the white surface, the Cyton has four layers of copper: one on top, two inside, and one on the bottom.

41
00:03:57,710 --> 00:04:01,280
The copper traces are the wires from the schematic.

42
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:08,770
Switch the 3D explorer to copper routing, and you can see them: red for the top layer, cyan for the bottom.

43
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:13,810
Tiny plated holes, called vias, carry signals from one layer to another.

44
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,005
Chapter recap.

45
00:04:16,005 --> 00:04:25,766
On top: the electrode header, the protection resistors, the ADS1299, the PIC32 with its crystal, buttons and LED, and the black sockets.

46
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:32,728
Underneath: the battery plug, the power chips, the radio, the SD card, and more protection parts.

47
00:04:32,728 --> 00:04:36,460
Every part has a name you can find in the schematic.

48
00:04:40,767 --> 00:04:44,637
Everything starts at the battery plug, B1, on the underside.

49
00:04:44,637 --> 00:04:49,345
The Cyton runs from a battery of 3 to 6 volts only, never from the mains.

50
00:04:49,345 --> 00:04:55,537
That's a safety choice: anything connected to your head should be isolated from the wall socket.

51
00:04:56,733 --> 00:05:00,329
The first part after the battery is D9, a diode.

52
00:05:00,329 --> 00:05:03,251
A diode is a one-way valve for current.

53
00:05:03,251 --> 00:05:09,020
If you plug the battery in backwards, D9 blocks it, and nothing gets damaged.

54
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:12,391
The price is a small drop of about 0.3 volts.

55
00:05:12,391 --> 00:05:14,563
What comes out is called RAW.

56
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:18,570
RAW goes to U5, a 3.3 volt regulator.

57
00:05:18,570 --> 00:05:26,696
A regulator takes a voltage that wanders, as the battery drains, and turns it into a perfectly steady one.

58
00:05:26,696 --> 00:05:38,041
Its output, DVDD, powers all the digital parts: the microcontroller, the radio, the accelerometer, the SD card, and the digital side of the ADS1299.

59
00:05:38,041 --> 00:05:43,637
A nice detail: the power switch doesn't carry the board's current at all.

60
00:05:43,637 --> 00:05:50,843
It only drives U5's enable pin, which switches the regulator, and the whole board, on and off.

61
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:57,044
The ADS1299's analog side needs two more supplies.

62
00:05:57,044 --> 00:06:00,727
U4 makes plus 2.5 volts, called AVDD.

63
00:06:00,727 --> 00:06:07,297
It's fed from the already clean 3.3 volts, so AVDD is extra quiet.

64
00:06:08,467 --> 00:06:15,527
The analog side also needs minus 2.5 volts, and a battery can't give you a negative voltage directly.

65
00:06:15,527 --> 00:06:16,645
That's U3's job.

66
00:06:16,645 --> 00:06:27,410
It's a charge pump: thousands of times a second, it charges a small capacitor from 3.3 volts, flips it upside down, and pours that charge into its output.

67
00:06:27,410 --> 00:06:29,927
The result is about minus 3.3 volts.

68
00:06:31,133 --> 00:06:40,746
That flipped voltage is a bit rippled, so VR1, a negative regulator, cleans it into a steady minus 2.5 volts, called AVSS.

69
00:06:40,746 --> 00:06:44,292
Now the ADS1299 has plus and minus 2.5 volts:

70
00:06:44,292 --> 00:06:46,971
5 volts in total, centred on zero.

71
00:06:46,971 --> 00:06:56,583
Centring on zero means the brain signal, which swings both ways around zero, sits right in the middle of the chip's range.

72
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,048
Next to every regulator you'll see small capacitors.

73
00:07:01,048 --> 00:07:09,605
Think of them as little water tanks: when a chip suddenly gulps current, the capacitor supplies it instantly, so the voltage doesn't dip.

74
00:07:09,605 --> 00:07:12,166
Regulators also need them to stay stable.

75
00:07:12,166 --> 00:07:17,350
In the schematic, this whole chain sits in the power supply block, at the top left.

76
00:07:18,533 --> 00:07:20,696
Why so many separate supplies?

77
00:07:20,696 --> 00:07:23,004
Because digital chips are noisy.

78
00:07:23,004 --> 00:07:32,305
Every time the microcontroller or the radio switches, it pulls quick spikes of current, and those spikes ripple along its supply.

79
00:07:32,305 --> 00:07:40,669
If the ADS1299's analog side shared that supply, the ripple would land right on top of your microvolt brain signals.

80
00:07:40,669 --> 00:07:50,908
So the analog side gets its own, extra-clean rails, AVDD and AVSS, made by their own low-noise regulators, while the digital parts share DVDD.

81
00:07:50,908 --> 00:07:56,243
Keeping analog and digital apart is one of the golden rules of EEG design.

82
00:07:57,467 --> 00:07:58,722
Chapter recap.

83
00:07:58,722 --> 00:08:01,950
Battery, then D9, the one-way valve.

84
00:08:01,950 --> 00:08:06,165
Then U5 makes 3.3 volts for everything digital.

85
00:08:06,165 --> 00:08:15,043
U4 makes plus 2.5 volts, and U3 with VR1 makes minus 2.5 volts, for the analog side of the ADS1299.

86
00:08:15,043 --> 00:08:19,437
And capacitors everywhere, as little water tanks.

87
00:08:23,767 --> 00:08:26,248
Now the main event: the brain signal.

88
00:08:26,248 --> 00:08:30,675
Each EEG electrode measures the voltage at one spot on your scalp.

89
00:08:30,675 --> 00:08:41,071
But a voltage only means something compared with another point, so every channel is measured against a shared reference electrode, usually on your earlobe.

90
00:08:41,071 --> 00:08:44,157
That reference connects to the pin called SRB.

91
00:08:45,333 --> 00:08:48,311
The electrode wires plug into the header.

92
00:08:48,311 --> 00:08:52,088
Each channel has two pins, P and N, in the two rows.

93
00:08:52,088 --> 00:08:57,898
Then there's SRB, the reference, and BIAS, which we'll meet in the next chapter.

94
00:08:57,898 --> 00:09:04,143
At the two ends sit small sockets for the analog supplies, which the Daisy uses later.

95
00:09:05,333 --> 00:09:10,927
From each pin, the signal first meets a TVS diode, on the underside, right below the pins.

96
00:09:10,927 --> 00:09:13,662
TVS stands for transient voltage suppressor.

97
00:09:13,662 --> 00:09:15,651
Normally it does nothing at all.

98
00:09:15,651 --> 00:09:26,093
But if you touch the pins with a static charge of thousands of volts, it switches on in nanoseconds and dumps that zap to ground, before it can reach the delicate chip.

99
00:09:27,267 --> 00:09:30,677
Next, through the board to the top: a 2.2 kΩ resistor.

100
00:09:30,677 --> 00:09:34,656
Four resistors are packed into each of these tiny black arrays.

101
00:09:34,656 --> 00:09:41,477
If something still gets past the diode, the resistor limits how much current can flow into the chip's input.

102
00:09:42,667 --> 00:09:47,226
Then back underneath: a 1 nF capacitor from each line to ground.

103
00:09:47,226 --> 00:09:53,423
Together with the 2.2 kΩ resistor, it forms a filter with a cutoff around 72 kilohertz.

104
00:09:53,423 --> 00:10:01,473
Brain signals are below 100 hertz, so they pass untouched, but radio noise from Wi-Fi and phones is drained away.

105
00:10:01,473 --> 00:10:06,887
Notice the zig-zag: diode underneath, resistor on top, capacitor underneath.

106
00:10:06,887 --> 00:10:09,737
The signal hops through the board twice.

107
00:10:10,933 --> 00:10:13,590
And now the signal reaches the ADS1299.

108
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:24,490
For each of the 8 channels, the chip first takes the difference between the electrode and the reference, so the hum your whole body carries largely cancels out.

109
00:10:24,490 --> 00:10:29,463
Then a programmable gain amplifier multiplies that tiny difference by 24.

110
00:10:30,667 --> 00:10:32,740
Why only 24, and not 1,000?

111
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:39,419
Because electrodes also carry a slowly drifting offset, up to a few hundred millivolts.

112
00:10:39,419 --> 00:10:47,327
With a gain of 24, the chip's input range is plus or minus 187 millivolts, wide enough for that offset.

113
00:10:47,327 --> 00:10:53,469
And its 24-bit converter is so fine that one step is only about 0.02 microvolts.

114
00:10:53,469 --> 00:10:59,227
So we get both: room for the offset, and detail far below the brain signal.

115
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:03,827
Next comes the analog-to-digital converter.

116
00:11:03,827 --> 00:11:11,637
All 8 channels are converted at exactly the same instant, 250 times a second, into 24-bit numbers.

117
00:11:11,637 --> 00:11:25,743
The converter needs a ruler to measure against: a 4.5 volt reference made inside the chip, held steady by capacitors on its VREF and VCAP pins, including C2, the big 100 µF one.

118
00:11:25,743 --> 00:11:34,350
When a new set of 8 samples is ready, the chip pulls a pin called DRDY, data ready, to say: come and get it.

119
00:11:35,533 --> 00:11:37,820
The ADS1299 has a few more tricks.

120
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:42,931
In front of each channel is a set of switches, called the input multiplexer.

121
00:11:42,931 --> 00:11:45,285
Normally it connects the electrode.

122
00:11:45,285 --> 00:11:59,407
But the firmware can also short a channel's inputs together to measure the chip's own noise, feed in a built-in test square wave to check everything works, or measure the chip's temperature and supply voltages.

123
00:11:59,407 --> 00:12:10,033
It also decides how the reference is used: with SRB2, the shared reference goes to each channel's P input separately, which is what the Cyton uses by default.

124
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:13,193
With SRB1, it goes to all the N inputs at once.

125
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:17,112
It can even check your electrodes.

126
00:12:17,112 --> 00:12:24,289
The chip sends a tiny current, 6 nanoamps, through each electrode, wobbling at 31.2 hertz.

127
00:12:24,289 --> 00:12:33,381
From the voltage that this creates, software works out how well each electrode touches your skin, using Ohm's law.

128
00:12:33,381 --> 00:12:37,049
That's the impedance check in the OpenBCI GUI.

129
00:12:37,049 --> 00:12:43,190
A good contact has low impedance; a poor one shows up as a big, noisy signal.

130
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:51,535
Finally, look at all the small capacitors gathered around the ADS1299, many of them right underneath it.

131
00:12:51,535 --> 00:12:59,973
Every power pin of the chip gets its own: usually a 1 µF and a 0.1 µF side by side, placed as close to the pin as possible.

132
00:12:59,973 --> 00:13:03,815
The big one stores energy, the small one reacts fastest.

133
00:13:03,815 --> 00:13:11,430
Without them, the chip's own switching would make its supply bounce, and that bounce would show up in your EEG.

134
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:13,893
Chapter recap.

135
00:13:13,893 --> 00:13:25,065
Electrode, then a TVS diode against static, a 2.2 kΩ resistor to limit current, and a 1 nF capacitor against radio noise.

136
00:13:25,065 --> 00:13:35,960
Then the ADS1299: subtract the reference, multiply by 24, convert to 24 bits, 250 times a second, and ring data ready.

137
00:13:40,233 --> 00:13:42,565
There's one problem left: mains hum.

138
00:13:42,565 --> 00:13:49,236
The power lines in your walls create a 50 or 60 hertz field, and your body picks it up like an antenna.

139
00:13:49,236 --> 00:13:53,446
That hum can be thousands of times bigger than your brain signal.

140
00:13:53,446 --> 00:13:57,203
Subtracting the reference removes most of it, but not all.

141
00:13:57,203 --> 00:13:59,923
The bias loop pushes it down even further.

142
00:14:01,133 --> 00:14:05,514
Inside the ADS1299, the electrode signals are averaged together.

143
00:14:05,514 --> 00:14:10,920
Brain signals differ from place to place, so they mostly cancel in the average.

144
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:14,411
What's left is the hum that every electrode shares.

145
00:14:14,411 --> 00:14:23,513
A built-in bias amplifier then drives the opposite of that hum back into your body, through a separate electrode: the bias electrode.

146
00:14:24,733 --> 00:14:32,045
On its way out, the bias drive passes R5, a 1 MΩ resistor, with C16, a 1 nF capacitor, across it.

147
00:14:32,045 --> 00:14:38,527
R5 limits the current into your body to a couple of microamps, even in the worst case.

148
00:14:38,527 --> 00:14:42,522
C16 lets faster changes, like the hum, get around R5.

149
00:14:42,522 --> 00:14:50,965
One detail from the schematic: the amplifier's minus input, BIAS_INV, isn't wired to anything else on the board.

150
00:14:50,965 --> 00:14:55,713
It's simply brought out to two pins, J2, so you can experiment.

151
00:14:56,933 --> 00:15:09,747
Then the drive passes a 2.2 kΩ protection resistor and a TVS diode, like every other line, and leaves through the BIAS pin to the bias electrode, often on the other earlobe or the forehead.

152
00:15:09,747 --> 00:15:16,323
The loop closes through you: the hum goes up on your body, and the amplifier pushes it back down.

153
00:15:16,323 --> 00:15:18,153
Every channel gets cleaner.

154
00:15:19,333 --> 00:15:20,445
Chapter recap.

155
00:15:20,445 --> 00:15:31,877
The ADS1299 averages the electrodes to find the shared hum, inverts it, and drives it back into your body through R5, R6 and the bias electrode.

156
00:15:31,877 --> 00:15:34,893
The hum on every channel gets smaller.

157
00:15:39,167 --> 00:15:40,788
Now the digital side.

158
00:15:40,788 --> 00:15:46,500
U7 is a PIC32 microcontroller: a tiny computer running OpenBCI's firmware.

159
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:58,157
Its job is to collect samples from the ADS1299 and the accelerometer, pack them into packets, and hand them to the radio, or write them to the SD card.

160
00:15:59,333 --> 00:16:08,059
It keeps time with X1, an 8 megahertz crystal: a sliver of quartz that vibrates at an exact frequency.

161
00:16:08,059 --> 00:16:14,304
The two tiny capacitors next to it, 18 pF each, help it start and settle.

162
00:16:14,304 --> 00:16:18,924
The chip multiplies this beat up to run its processor.

163
00:16:18,924 --> 00:16:29,703
Don't confuse it with the ADS1299's own clock: that one runs at 2.048 megahertz, inside the ADS1299, and it times the samples.

164
00:16:30,933 --> 00:16:37,355
The microcontroller talks to the ADS1299 over SPI, a simple four-wire link.

165
00:16:37,355 --> 00:16:41,208
The microcontroller drives a clock wire, SCK.

166
00:16:41,208 --> 00:16:47,544
On each tick, one bit travels out on MOSI, and one bit comes back on MISO.

167
00:16:47,544 --> 00:16:52,510
A fourth wire, chip-select, picks which chip is listening.

168
00:16:52,510 --> 00:17:00,902
The SPI wires are shared with the accelerometer and the SD card, and each has its own chip-select:

169
00:17:00,902 --> 00:17:08,693
CS1 for the ADS1299, CS3 for the SD card, CS4 for the accelerometer, and CS2 for the Daisy.

170
00:17:09,867 --> 00:17:13,680
Every 4 milliseconds, the ADS1299 pulls DRDY low.

171
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:18,661
That's a doorbell, wired to an interrupt on the microcontroller.

172
00:17:18,661 --> 00:17:22,785
It immediately pulls CS1 low and clocks out 27 bytes:

173
00:17:22,785 --> 00:17:27,377
3 bytes of status, then 3 bytes for each of the 8 channels.

174
00:17:28,600 --> 00:17:31,476
Two buttons control the microcontroller.

175
00:17:31,476 --> 00:17:35,430
RST pulls its reset pin, MCLR, to ground to restart it.

176
00:17:35,430 --> 00:17:39,672
A 470 kΩ resistor holds that pin high the rest of the time.

177
00:17:39,672 --> 00:17:45,928
PROG, held while you reset, tells it to start its bootloader and wait for new firmware.

178
00:17:45,928 --> 00:17:49,307
And the blue LED, D1, shows the board's status.

179
00:17:49,307 --> 00:17:58,510
In the schematic, the microcontroller block shows all of this: chip-selects, crystal, buttons, and the serial link to the radio.

180
00:17:59,733 --> 00:18:09,351
The PIC32 comes with a chipKIT bootloader, a small program that lets you load new firmware over USB through the dongle, using the Arduino software.

181
00:18:09,351 --> 00:18:19,753
Its spare pins are brought out on the two black sockets, J3 and J4, so you can connect your own add-ons, like a button or a trigger signal from another device.

182
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:22,220
Chapter recap.

183
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:27,183
The PIC32 runs the firmware on an 8 megahertz crystal.

184
00:18:27,183 --> 00:18:43,083
It talks SPI to the ADS1299, the accelerometer, the SD card and the Daisy, each with its own chip-select, and it reads 27 bytes from the ADS1299 every time data ready rings.

185
00:18:47,367 --> 00:18:59,220
Each packet the microcontroller builds is 33 bytes long: a start byte, a sample number, 24 bytes of channel data, 6 bytes of accelerometer data, and a stop byte.

186
00:18:59,220 --> 00:19:05,847
It sends each packet over a serial link to BLE1, an RFduino radio module on the underside.

187
00:19:07,067 --> 00:19:14,513
The RFduino transmits each packet wirelessly to the OpenBCI USB dongle, plugged into your computer.

188
00:19:14,513 --> 00:19:18,123
Every 4 milliseconds, a packet leaves the board.

189
00:19:18,123 --> 00:19:28,427
On the computer, software such as the OpenBCI GUI or BrainFlow turns the 24-bit numbers back into microvolts, and plots your brain waves.

190
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:40,523
The radio can also do something clever: it can reprogram the microcontroller over the air, using the programming pins, so you can update the firmware without any cables.

191
00:19:40,523 --> 00:19:42,462
And remember the slide switch?

192
00:19:42,462 --> 00:19:46,146
In the BLE position, it tells the radio to use Bluetooth.

193
00:19:46,146 --> 00:19:56,810
That signal comes straight from the battery, so it passes through D8 and a resistor, and a Zener diode, D10, clamps it to about 3 volts to protect the radio's input.

194
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,385
Let's do the maths on the data.

195
00:20:00,385 --> 00:20:06,077
33 bytes per packet, 250 packets a second, makes 8,250 bytes every second.

196
00:20:06,077 --> 00:20:15,923
The serial link between the microcontroller and the radio runs at 115,200 bits per second, which is about 11,500 bytes a second.

197
00:20:15,923 --> 00:20:20,615
So the link is already about 70 percent full with 8 channels.

198
00:20:20,615 --> 00:20:28,230
That's exactly why 16 channels can't simply be sent at 250 per second, as we'll see with the Daisy.

199
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:30,434
Chapter recap.

200
00:20:30,434 --> 00:20:39,520
A 33-byte packet, 250 times a second, over a serial link to the RFduino, then by radio to the USB dongle and your computer.

201
00:20:43,833 --> 00:20:49,139
The accelerometer, U1, measures how your head tilts and moves, in three directions.

202
00:20:49,139 --> 00:20:56,043
Its readings go into the 6 aux bytes of each packet, so software can spot movement that might spoil the EEG.

203
00:20:57,267 --> 00:21:08,275
It shares the SPI wires, but this chip can also speak another protocol, I²C, and busy SPI traffic could accidentally look like an I²C start signal to it.

204
00:21:08,275 --> 00:21:13,599
So U2, a tiny OR gate, holds its data pin high whenever it isn't selected.

205
00:21:13,599 --> 00:21:19,787
It's like an earplug that's only removed when the microcontroller wants to talk to it.

206
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:28,295
The micro SD card slot, on the underside, lets the board record without a computer at all.

207
00:21:28,295 --> 00:21:32,996
It uses the same SPI wires, with its own chip-select, CS3.

208
00:21:32,996 --> 00:21:39,480
C4, a big 100 µF capacitor, covers the card's bursts of current while it writes.

209
00:21:40,667 --> 00:21:41,713
Chapter recap.

210
00:21:41,713 --> 00:21:47,544
The accelerometer adds motion to every packet, with an OR gate as its earplug.

211
00:21:47,544 --> 00:21:51,207
And the SD card lets the board record on its own.

212
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:57,768
Now let's add the Daisy module.

213
00:21:57,768 --> 00:22:03,915
It's a second, smaller board that sits on top of the Cyton and adds 8 more channels:

214
00:22:03,915 --> 00:22:04,500
9 to 16.

215
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:14,013
It has its own ADS1299, its own protection parts and its own electrode header, but no microcontroller, no radio and no regulators.

216
00:22:14,013 --> 00:22:16,720
Everything else comes from the Cyton.

217
00:22:17,933 --> 00:22:20,176
Here's the Daisy on its own.

218
00:22:20,176 --> 00:22:38,119
On top: its electrode header along the top edge, five resistor arrays under it, the ADS1299 in the middle, the 8-pin Daisy bus on the left edge, and two pads, J2, for the bias amplifier's minus input, just like on the Cyton.

219
00:22:38,119 --> 00:22:38,000
Underneath:

220
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:46,049
TVS diodes and capacitor arrays below the pins, and the ADS1299's decoupling capacitors.

221
00:22:46,049 --> 00:22:49,653
Same building blocks, minus the digital side.

222
00:22:50,867 --> 00:22:57,710
Both chips are identical, with identical settings: gain 24, 24-bit, 250 samples a second.

223
00:22:57,710 --> 00:23:04,477
The Cyton's chip handles channels 1 to 8, and the Daisy's chip handles channels 9 to 16.

224
00:23:05,667 --> 00:23:13,831
The extra electrodes plug into the Daisy's own right-angle header, which sits right above the Cyton's.

225
00:23:13,831 --> 00:23:16,632
Each line gets the same protection:

226
00:23:16,632 --> 00:23:24,797
TVS diode underneath, 2.2 kΩ resistor on top, 1 nF capacitor underneath, and then the Daisy's ADS1299.

227
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,540
The boards talk through 8 pins.

228
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:33,619
The Daisy's pins point down into J1, the Cyton's black socket:

229
00:23:33,619 --> 00:23:42,630
3.3 volt power, ground, the three shared SPI wires, reset, a clock line, and CS2, the Daisy's own chip-select.

230
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:46,179
Analog power arrives another way.

231
00:23:46,179 --> 00:23:52,740
At the two ends of the Cyton's electrode header sit small 2-hole sockets for AVSS and AVDD.

232
00:23:52,740 --> 00:24:01,680
The Daisy's matching pins plug straight down into them, so both chips run on the very same plus and minus 2.5 volt supplies.

233
00:24:02,867 --> 00:24:05,473
So how do two chips stay in sync?

234
00:24:05,473 --> 00:24:06,816
First: one clock.

235
00:24:06,816 --> 00:24:12,978
The Daisy's clock-select pin is tied to ground, so it has no clock of its own.

236
00:24:12,978 --> 00:24:23,484
When the firmware finds a Daisy, it turns on the Cyton chip's clock output, and both chips count the very same 2.048 megahertz ticks.

237
00:24:23,484 --> 00:24:29,567
8,192 ticks make one sample, so both finish every sample at the same instant.

238
00:24:30,733 --> 00:24:31,996
Second: one start.

239
00:24:31,996 --> 00:24:40,482
The microcontroller pulls both chip-selects low together and sends a single START command, which both chips hear at once.

240
00:24:40,482 --> 00:24:43,848
A shared reset line restarts them together, too.

241
00:24:43,848 --> 00:24:47,985
At start-up, it also reads the Daisy chip's ID through CS2.

242
00:24:47,985 --> 00:24:52,193
If the answer is 0x3E, the firmware switches to 16 channels.

243
00:24:53,400 --> 00:24:54,876
Third: one doorbell.

244
00:24:54,876 --> 00:24:58,937
The Daisy's data-ready pin isn't connected to anything.

245
00:24:58,937 --> 00:25:09,420
When the Cyton chip rings data ready, the Daisy's sample is ready too, so the microcontroller reads 27 bytes with CS1, then 27 bytes with CS2.

246
00:25:10,600 --> 00:25:15,065
The radio link can't carry 16 channels 250 times a second.

247
00:25:15,065 --> 00:25:24,689
So the firmware averages each pair of samples and takes turns: odd packets carry channels 1 to 8, even packets carry 9 to 16.

248
00:25:24,689 --> 00:25:31,310
Each channel arrives 125 times a second, and the software stitches them back together.

249
00:25:32,533 --> 00:25:33,999
Two practical notes.

250
00:25:33,999 --> 00:25:38,910
The Daisy has its own SRB and BIAS pins, separate from the Cyton's.

251
00:25:38,910 --> 00:25:45,361
For one reference across all 16 channels, join the two SRB pins with a Y-splitter cable.

252
00:25:45,361 --> 00:25:54,963
The Daisy's bias drive goes to its own BIAS pin: you can leave it unused, and let the Cyton's bias electrode serve all 16 channels.

253
00:25:56,133 --> 00:25:57,257
Chapter recap.

254
00:25:57,257 --> 00:26:01,512
The Daisy adds a second ADS1299 for channels 9 to 16.

255
00:26:01,512 --> 00:26:10,344
It gets 3.3 volts and SPI through the 8-pin Daisy bus, and plus and minus 2.5 volts through two small sockets.

256
00:26:10,344 --> 00:26:18,613
One clock, one start, and one doorbell keep both chips in step, and the radio takes turns between them.

257
00:26:22,900 --> 00:26:27,222
Let's put it all together, with every flow running at once.

258
00:26:27,222 --> 00:26:31,690
Power: battery, D9, 3.3 volts, then plus and minus 2.5 volts.

259
00:26:31,690 --> 00:26:35,499
Signal: electrode, protection, amplifier, converter.

260
00:26:35,499 --> 00:26:38,428
Bias: the hum, inverted and pushed back.

261
00:26:38,428 --> 00:26:42,018
Control: the microcontroller, SPI and data ready.

262
00:26:42,018 --> 00:26:45,973
Radio: a packet every 4 milliseconds to your computer.

263
00:26:45,973 --> 00:26:51,320
And the Daisy, locked in step with one clock, one start and one doorbell.

264
00:26:52,533 --> 00:26:56,666
Now follow one single sample through its 4 milliseconds.

265
00:26:56,666 --> 00:27:02,716
Your brain makes a tiny voltage; the electrode picks it up, together with the hum.

266
00:27:02,716 --> 00:27:08,989
The TVS diode, the resistor and the capacitor let it through, cleaned of radio noise.

267
00:27:08,989 --> 00:27:14,228
The ADS1299 subtracts the reference, multiplies by 24, and converts it.

268
00:27:14,228 --> 00:27:15,703
It rings data ready.

269
00:27:15,703 --> 00:27:21,459
The PIC32 reads 27 bytes, adds the accelerometer, and builds a 33-byte packet.

270
00:27:21,459 --> 00:27:23,894
The radio sends it to the dongle.

271
00:27:23,894 --> 00:27:32,528
And on your computer, each number is multiplied by about 0.02 microvolts, to give you back the voltage on your scalp.

272
00:27:32,528 --> 00:27:36,143
Then, 4 milliseconds later, it all happens again.

273
00:27:37,333 --> 00:27:43,166
For your own board, BrainTech-8, the same flows apply, with a few changes.

274
00:27:43,166 --> 00:27:49,394
An ESP32-S3 replaces both the PIC32 and the radio, since it has Wi-Fi built in.

275
00:27:49,394 --> 00:27:57,907
Its Wi-Fi bursts draw around 350 milliamps, so the 3.3 volt supply has to be much stronger than the Cyton's.

276
00:27:57,907 --> 00:28:10,913
You're also planning 47 pF capacitors on the inputs instead of 1 nF, two 100 kΩ resistors in series with the bias electrode, and a lithium battery with USB charging.

277
00:28:10,913 --> 00:28:20,293
Everything else, the protection, the ADS1299 with its capacitors, and the bias loop, you now understand from the Cyton.

278
00:28:21,467 --> 00:28:25,321
That's the Cyton and the Daisy, from battery to brain waves.

279
00:28:25,321 --> 00:28:33,992
Keep the 3D explorer open while you draw your own schematic: click any part to see where its signal comes from, and where it goes next.

280
00:28:33,992 --> 00:28:35,727
Good luck with BrainTech-8.
